The cause of the failures is that the wired context was never passed to `subscribe`. The first version destructured `{ data }` from the wire value, which is `undefined` because the MessageContext wire delivers the context object itself. The second version used a property wire. Assigning a property that the template doesn't use doesn't re-render the component, so `renderedCallback` never ran and the subscription was never created.

This version uses a method wire that receives the context directly and subscribes at that point. `connectedCallback` also subscribes, which covers the case where the context is already cached when the component is re-inserted. The subscription guard keeps at most one handle, and `disconnectedCallback` releases it.

